.Im MicroEmacs variable|file
.so me.tni
.TH $file-checks 5
.XI $file-checks - "File save check actions"
.SH NAME
$file-checks \- File save check actions
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB$file-checks\fR \fIbitmask\fR; Default is \fC0\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reading a file is lossless, so a condition which may need the user's attention is reported in the read message, e.g.
"\fC[Read 120 lines - 2 NULs, mixed line endings, no final newline]\fR", and checked again when the buffer is saved
with
.Ht save-buffer 2
or
.Ht write-buffer 2 ,
so changes made since the file was read count. The user is prompted for each condition still present, check \fB5\fR
first as text would be lost, then in the order below, to preserve it or to fix the buffer before it is written, and
an abort (\fCC-g\fR) aborts the save. A preserve answer to checks \fB1\fR to \fB4\fR is remembered for the buffer
until the file is read again. The conditions are:
.IP "\fB1\fR NUL characters"
The file is binary, the NULs may be preserved or stripped from the buffer. A version of \*(mS without Unicode support
removes the NULs when the file is read and asks whether the file should be written without them, this check's bits
are not used.
.IP "\fB2\fR Inconsistent line endings"
Lines end with different line endings, e.g. some with a CR and LF and others with only an LF. The line endings may be
preserved, each line being written with its own ending, or standardised to Unix LF or Windows CRLF line endings, see
the
.Ht cr 2m
and
.Ht lf 2m
modes.
.IP "\fB3\fR No final newline"
The last line has no line ending, a newline may be added or this preserved.
.IP "\fB4\fR Invalid bytes"
A UTF-8, UTF-16 or UTF-32 file has bytes which are not valid in its encoding (see
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 ),
e.g. stray bytes of a code page in a UTF-8 file. They may be preserved, converted to the characters the current
code page gives them or replaced with the replacement character U+FFFD. This check is only made by a version of \*(mS
with Unicode support.
.IP "\fB5\fR Characters the code page does not have"
The buffer is saved in a code page (see
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 )
and holds characters the code page does not have, typically text pasted or typed since the file was read, e.g. Greek
in a \fCcp1252\fR file. They cannot be written, so they may be replaced with a '\fC?\fR' or the file converted to
UTF-8, which changes the buffer's
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 .
To use another code page, abort the save and change the buffer's encoding. This check is always asked first, and as
the text is lost a replace answer is never remembered. This check is only made by a version of \*(mS with Unicode
support.
.PP
\fB$file-checks\fR sets the action of each check, 2 bits per check starting from bit 0, so check \fB1\fR uses bits
\fC0x03\fR, \fB2\fR uses \fC0x0c\fR, \fB3\fR \fC0x30\fR, \fB4\fR \fC0xc0\fR and \fB5\fR \fC0x300\fR, where:
.IP \fC0\fR
Prompt the user (the default).
.IP \fC1\fR
Always preserve.
.IP \fC2\fR
Always fix, stripping the NULs, standardising the line endings to the buffer's
.Ht cr 2m
and
.Ht lf 2m
modes, adding a newline, converting invalid bytes and converting the file to UTF-8.
.PP
For check \fB5\fR preserving the code page is the choice which loses text: \fC1\fR always replaces the characters with
a '\fC?\fR' and \fC2\fR always converts the file to UTF-8.
.PP
A save which does not check the file preserves every condition, writing a '\fC?\fR' for each character check \fB5\fR
would ask about, i.e. a
.Ht write-buffer 2
with an argument of \fC0\fR, a macro using the same argument,
.Ht save-some-buffers 2
and the saves made on exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
To always add a missing final newline without being prompted, check \fB3\fR is set to always fix:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable $file-checks 0x20
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 ,
.Ht $buffer-fmod 5 ,
.Ht cr 2m ,
.Ht lf 2m ,
.Ht save-buffer 2 ,
.Ht write-buffer 2 .
.ad
.FH
